### Authoring a backlog item → run this SOP BEFORE you enqueue an Add

A backlog item is a task spec in waiting: whatever you leave out here, a builder has to guess at spawn
time. Before you hand an Add to the scribe, walk these eight — the first that fails sends you back to the
repo/journal, not to the scribe:

1. **GROUNDED.** The gap is real — verified against **live code / the journal**, not memory. Cite the
   dated evidence (a file+line, a `herd why <pr>` line, a journal event) so a stale or already-shipped
   idea never gets filed. If you can't point at the gap, research it first (research lane) — don't file.
2. **SHAPE.** A short **title line** (< 80 chars) FIRST, then the body — the tracker takes line 1 as the
   title. Never open with a run-on paragraph (that files a giant duplicated title).
3. **FIX + SURFACE.** The body names a **fix sketch AND its file surface** — the exact scripts/functions
   the change lands in. "Fix the watcher race" is not a spec; "guard the sha-cache write in
   `agent-watch.sh` gate loop" is.
4. **SEQUENCING.** Call out **explicit conflicts with in-flight / queued items** — same file surface,
   dependent order, or a rename that would dangle it. Cross it against `git worktree list` + the open
   queue (and the pre-spawn graphify read) so it isn't filed into a collision.
5. **VERIFICATION PLAN.** Decide up front **how the change will be proven**, and write it into the item
   so it flows into the builder task spec at spawn. If it touches a **gate / merge / concurrency /
   limit-park / pane** seam, name the exact **sandbox sim scenario + the seam** it exercises (see
   *Verify GATE / MERGE …* above) — a green scorecard is the proof. Otherwise name the **test surface**
   (which `tests/*.sh` or unit covers it). An item with no verification plan ships the builder blind.
   Where `CORE_SURFACE_GLOB` is armed this is not optional for a matching file surface: ask
   `{{SCRIPTS_DIR}}/core-surface.sh scenarios <worktree>` and write the named scenario + the
   serialization warning into the spec (see *The CORE SURFACE makes that requirement automatic*).
6. **CONVENTIONS.** Bake in the engine defaults: **fail-soft** (a missing optional tool skips silently,
   never a red row), **ship-dormant / default-off** (new behavior rides behind a config key, off by
   default), and **byte-identical-when-off** (with the flag off the rendered/observed output is
   unchanged). Say which apply so the builder doesn't have to infer them.
7. **MULTI-SEAT / INVARIANCE.** Many coordinator seats work this repo in parallel, so ask both halves:
   does the behavior hold **regardless of which seat performs the triggering event** (prefer an
   invariant reconciled every tick against observed state over an event side-effect that only fires
   for the seat that acted — a codemap refreshed only inside `do_merge` goes stale after another
   seat's merge), and is any new check **a single shared implementation reused at every enforcement
   surface** (the builder's pre-PR light profile AND the merge gate — a gate-only check the builder
   can't run just bounces the PR; prose the builder must judge is not a check). Seat-local state and
   merge-performer-dependent behavior are correctness defects. See `docs/multi-seat-doctrine.md`.
8. **PROGRAM.** Ask whether this item belongs to a **bigger effort**. If it does, the body's last line is
   a one-line backlink — `Part of <epic> — Phase N; full context: <committed doc path>` — so a builder
   (and the next seat) reaches the goal and the evidence without a session paste. If you are about to
   file the **third** item sharing one root cause and no epic exists, **create the epic FIRST**, then
   file this item as its child (file-then-spawn discipline: the epic exists before any builder is
   spawned against it). Long-form evidence belongs in a **committed doc** — `docs/audits/` for
   post-mortems, `docs/` for designs — never only in a session artifact, and items cite that path.
   See the *Programs* section of `docs/COORDINATOR-SOP.md`.

Keep every backlog edit **privacy-safe** — nothing under `{{DENY_PATHS}}` and no secrets ever
reach `{{BACKLOG_FILE}}` (it's committed).

## Switching work-tracker backends

When the operator asks to move work tracking (markdown file ↔ GitHub Issues ↔ Linear), do NOT
hand-edit `.herd/config` or `.herd/secrets` — run the one guided command and relay its prompts:

    herd backend switch <file|github|linear|changelog> [--migrate|--no-migrate]

It preflights credentials BEFORE changing anything (linear: asks for the API key on a tty, stores
it in `.herd/secrets` — gitignored, never in config — and proves it with a live API round-trip;
github: checks `gh auth`), flips `SCRIBE_BACKEND` through the validated config-set path, offers to
replay the old backend's open items into the new one (idempotent; titles get a
"(migrated from …)" provenance suffix), stamps `{{BACKLOG_FILE}}` as a FROZEN ARCHIVE when leaving
the file backend, restarts the backlog pane, and journals `backend_switched`. Everything downstream
— the scribe drainer, `herd backlog`, the watcher's mark-shipped, this skill — follows
`SCRIBE_BACKEND` automatically. Two operator-facing footnotes to pass along: retire any idle scribe
drainer spawned under the OLD backend (the flip warns about this), and secrets NEVER go in
`.herd/config` or the backlog file.

On `linear`, when the API key's workspace hosts more than one team's issues, set `LINEAR_TEAM_ID`
(`herd config set LINEAR_TEAM_ID <id>`, or alongside `LINEAR_API_KEY` in `.herd/secrets`) so
`herd backlog` and new-issue creation are scoped to just this project's team instead of every team
the key can see. It never scopes resolving an EXISTING reference (mark-shipped, claim, a
cross-team `blocked-on:` dep) — that always resolves by the identifier's own team prefix. Unset is
byte-identical to today.

## Workflow settings

Every workflow preference lives in `.herd/config`, and the user can view or change **any** of them
at **any** time — nothing is init-only. Drive it entirely through the validated CLI; never hand-edit
`.herd/config` yourself:

    herd config list              # print every effective key + value (secrets masked)
    herd config get <KEY>         # read one key
    herd config set <KEY> <VALUE> # change one key

When the user picks **Workflow settings**, start with `herd config list` so they see the current
state, then interview them (AskUserQuestion) for what to change and apply it with `herd config set`.
Surface what the CLI guarantees so the user knows the change is safe and live:

- **Validated keys** — `set` refuses any key not in the capabilities manifest, so a typo can't
  silently write dead config.
- **Secrets stay out** — secret-shaped keys are masked in `list`/`get` and refused by `set`;
  credentials belong in `.herd/secrets`, never the committed config.
- **Auto-applied, nothing init-only** — after a successful `set` the CLI *does what the change
  requires*: a **watcher-affecting key** restarts the watcher (via `herd reload`) and a
  **coordinator-facing key** re-renders this coordinator skill. A key needing neither is just
  written. **Watcher-affecting changes take effect on the watcher's restart** — the CLI prints
  which happened, so relay that back.

## Check running agents / review & merge

You own **all** backlog updates — sub-agents build + open PRs, the auto-merge watcher merges them.

1. `{{DRIVER_LIST_AGENTS}}` + `gh pr list` to match agents to open PRs. <!-- DRIVER:list-agents -->
2. **The watcher merges ready PRs automatically** — MERGEABLE+CLEAN → healthcheck + review gate →
   `gh pr merge`. Step in ONLY when the watcher surfaces **needs-you / review blocked / health
   failed**: a ❌ code error → send the agent back to fix it (⚠️ data/env is fine).
3. For a CONFLICTING PR, **do NOT hand-resolve it here** — spawn the isolated, test-gated resolver:
   `bash {{SCRIPTS_DIR}}/herd-resolve.sh <slug>`
   It merges the default branch in, fixes mechanical conflicts, verifies, and pushes — or aborts
   and prints `ESCALATE:` for a human. The watcher NEVER blind-merges a conflict.

### Builder notes — read, route, ack (HERD-202)

A builder that discovers a load-bearing mid-build finding — a red row that is a **stale cached
result**, a test file **not wired into the gate**, a hold that is **env, not code** — files it with
`herd note "<finding>"`. That journals a `builder_note` event and the watcher renders it in a
**builder notes** console section within a tick. You are that section's consumer: notes only reach
you if you drain them, and nothing else in the engine acts on one. **Do this every time you check
running agents, and once on invocation** — before you re-task a builder or override a gate, because a
note frequently explains the row you are about to act on.

1. **READ** the notes you have not handled yet:
   ```
   herd notes                     # numbered, newest first — the notes on the console right now
   herd log | grep builder_note   # fallback / full history, including already-handled notes
   ```
   `herd notes` is the ack-aware listing; if this engine build predates it, read the ledger the
   watcher renders from (`.agent-watch-builder-notes` in the worktree pool) or grep the journal.
2. **ROUTE** each note — exactly one of three, and say which you chose:
   - **Act now** — the note changes what you were about to do (don't bounce that builder; the red is
     a stale cache; the hold clears itself). Do the thing, then ack.
   - **File it** — the note names real work that outlives this build. Enqueue a tracker item through
     the scribe, quoting the note **verbatim** so the finding survives the summary:
     `bash {{SCRIPTS_DIR}}/scribe.sh "Add a 🔜 item: <short title> — builder <slug> reported: \"<note text, verbatim>\". <why it matters + fix sketch>"`
     Author it against the *Authoring a backlog item* SOP first. Then ack.
   - **Dismiss** — informational, no action (a builder narrating a decision). Ack it anyway.
3. **ACK** the handled note so the console clears and the next seat does not re-route it:
   ```
   herd notes ack <n>     # the number from `herd notes`
   herd notes ack all     # everything currently on the console
   ```
   The ack is display-only: `herd log` keeps every note forever, so acking never destroys evidence.
   **When your routing named an artifact you are still waiting on** — the item the scribe is filing,
   the PR a re-task will land — hand the *waiting* to the engine instead of holding it in your head
   (HERD-640, and this needs `INTENT_QUEUE=on`):
   ```
   herd notes route <n> item:HERD-123   # ack it once that item resolves in the tracker
   herd notes route <n> pr:812          # ack it once that PR lands
   ```
   This acks **nothing** now: it records the artifact, and the watcher clears the note the moment the
   artifact demonstrably exists. Routing stays your call — a **Dismiss** has no artifact, so it takes
   no `route`; ack it yourself.
   **If `herd notes ack` is not available in this engine build** (it ships with HERD-243), the loop
   still works — read and route exactly as above, and handled notes simply **age out of the display**
   on their own rather than clearing on command. Never skip the routing step just because you cannot
   ack: an unacked note is a note nobody has acted on.

### Diagnose a gate: "what happened / is it stuck / why did X fail" → `herd why` FIRST

When the user asks **what happened to a PR**, **why a PR is stuck**, or **why a gate / review / merge
failed**, your FIRST move is the engine journal — not process archaeology, not re-reading panes:

```
herd why <pr#>      # chronological gate history for ONE PR: every review dispatch, verdict (with
                    # reviewer/gate_default/infra provenance), healthcheck attempt + outcome, auto-refix
                    # bounce + wake result, and the merge/reap — the whole story in order
herd log --pr <n>   # the raw event stream for that PR; add --tail to watch gate activity live
```

The journal (`{{PROJECT_ROOT}}`'s worktree pool → `.herd/journal.jsonl`) is the append-only record
every engine component writes as it acts. It is authoritative for post-mortems: it captures the
things that used to vanish — a mid-review Claude death (`infra_event`), a verdict's provenance
(`verdict_recorded` source=`reviewer|gate_default|infra`), each healthcheck attempt/retry before a red
row, and an auto-refix bounce that silently failed to wake the builder (`refix_wake_result`
escalated=true). Read `herd why <pr>` before deciding whether to re-task a builder, override a block,
or escalate — then act on what the record actually shows.

### Auto-refix BLOCK reviews (REVIEW_AUTOFIX)

When `.herd/config` sets `REVIEW_AUTOFIX=true`, the watcher bounces each new BLOCK verdict
directly to the builder: it finds the builder's idle agent pane, sends a re-task prompt with
`gh pr view <n>` instructions to fix and push, and verifies the agent wakes within ~15 s (retrying
once). Console shows **refixing (round k/3)** while in flight; **fix in progress · awaiting push
(round k/3)** on subsequent ticks for the same sha; **needs you · auto-refix failed** if the agent
won't wake. Round cap (HERD-229): `REFIX_MAX_ROUNDS` (default 3) bounces per **rail** — review,
health and stale-base each budget separately — then **needs you**. A rail's counter is zeroed when
that rail's red resolves (a review PASS, a CLEAN suite, a freshened base): a converging loop is not a
loop. A per-PR total ceiling of `3 x REFIX_MAX_ROUNDS` across all rails still escalates a PR that
keeps failing everywhere. Sha-keyed semantics mirror review-once: one bounce per BLOCK sha; a new
commit resets the budget for that sha.

When `REVIEW_AUTOFIX=false` (default), the watcher shows the standard **review blocked** row and
you re-task builders by hand via `{{DRIVER_FOCUS_AGENT}}` or by messaging the agent pane. <!-- DRIVER:focus-agent -->

### Auto-heal stale-base holds (STALE_BASE_AUTOFIX)

When `.herd/config` sets `STALE_BASE_AUTOFIX=on`, a **stale-base** hold from the pre-merge
stale-duplicate gate (touched files moved on `$DEFAULT_BRANCH`) self-heals: the watcher re-tasks
the live builder with `git merge $DEFAULT_BRANCH` (or dispatches the conflict resolver when no live
builder remains). Console shows **rebasing · awaiting push**. Spends the **stale rail's own**
`REFIX_MAX_ROUNDS` budget (refunded once the base is fresh); only bounce-exhaustion escalates to
**needs you**. The **duplicate** flavor
(re-implements shipped work) always stays a human judgment call. Default is `off` (ship-dormant).

### Auto-refix a red healthcheck (HEALTHCHECK_AUTOFIX)

A reproduced pre-merge healthcheck **CODE ERROR** is the same shape of finding as a BLOCK review —
a machine-checkable defect in the builder's own diff — so `HEALTHCHECK_AUTOFIX=true` bounces it on
the same rails: the builder's agent pane receives the failing test line plus the path of the tailable
suite log, once per `(pr, sha)`. The round budget is the **health rail's own** — `REFIX_MAX_ROUNDS`
per rail, refunded when the suite next goes CLEAN — so a healthcheck red never eats the budget a later
review BLOCK needs, and the per-PR `3 x` total ceiling still stops a PR that thrashes across rails. A limit-parked builder is never typed at (it
routes to the park/resume handler, keeping its bounce for when it returns), a dead or missing agent
escalates without burning a round, and a `tab-leak-guard` trip is treated as the infra transient it is
(never bounced). Exhausting the budget escalates to **needs you · refix limit …**.

`HEALTHCHECK_AUTOFIX=false` (the default) bounces nothing: the red row holds the PR and you re-task
by hand.

### Row truth: "needs you" means nobody is on it

A red row says **needs you** only when *no agent is working that red* — and then it always carries
both the **blocker** (which test failed) and the **remedy** (what to do, and where the full suite
output lives). Whenever a builder is actively fixing it — because the watcher bounced it, or because
you re-tasked the agent by hand and its status reads `working` against that same red sha — the row
instead reads **fix in progress · awaiting push (round k/3)**. So a `needs you` on the console is
always real work for you, never work already in flight.

### Approve pending merges (MERGE_POLICY=approve)

When `.herd/config` sets `MERGE_POLICY=approve`, the watcher runs the full healthcheck + review
pipeline but holds before merging — posting a PR comment and a notification once per commit sha.
To approve and trigger merge:

```
bash {{SCRIPTS_DIR}}/herd-approve.sh list              # show gate-passed PRs awaiting approval
bash {{SCRIPTS_DIR}}/herd-approve.sh approve <pr#>     # write sha-keyed approval → watcher merges on next poll (~4 s)
```

Approval is **sha-keyed**: a new commit pushed after the awaiting record was written invalidates
the prior approval — the gate cycle re-runs and a fresh approval is required. The watcher shows
`ready · awaiting approval` in the status pane for any PR that has passed all gates but is not
yet approved.

## Verify GATE / MERGE / CONCURRENCY / LIMIT-PARK / PANE behavior → reproduce in the sim FIRST

When you are investigating or verifying **gate / merge / concurrency / limit-park / pane** behavior —
or before you **trust an engine change** that touches any of those seams — reproduce it in the matching
**sandbox sim scenario FIRST**, never against the live watcher. The sims run at **zero quota** (stub
builders, no model call) and reset to a **byte-identical** fixture, so every run is deterministic; the
concurrency / limit / pane scenarios drive the **shipped** watcher code in lib mode, so a green
scorecard is real signal — they break if that code regresses:

```
bash {{SCRIPTS_DIR}}/sim/sandbox-scenario.sh --artifacts /tmp/gate-run          # one-PR GATE / MERGE happy path + fault isolation
bash {{SCRIPTS_DIR}}/sim/sandbox-concurrency-scenario.sh --artifacts /tmp/conc  # the REAL watcher CONCURRENCY gate loop (N≥3 PRs)
bash {{SCRIPTS_DIR}}/sim/sandbox-limit-resume-scenario.sh --artifacts /tmp/lr   # LIMIT-PARK → auto-resume (+ HERD_LIMIT_DETECT=off negative seam)
bash {{SCRIPTS_DIR}}/sim/sandbox-real-panes-scenario.sh --artifacts /tmp/rp     # real disposable PANE control room + clean teardown
SANDBOX_FORCE_GATE_FAIL=1 bash {{SCRIPTS_DIR}}/sim/sandbox-scenario.sh --artifacts /tmp/fail   # fault-inject: gate fails LOUDLY, merge skipped
```

Each writes a machine-readable `scorecard.json` (`result: pass` iff `failed == 0`) — read it, don't
eyeball the console. `{{SCRIPTS_DIR}}/sim/README-sandbox-sim.md` catalogs the full tier table and each
scenario's invariants. **Thread this into the builder task spec whenever you spawn a watcher-touching
item** (anything under `agent-watch.sh`, the lanes, `herd-review.sh`, or gate / merge / limit logic):
tell the builder to reproduce the change in the matching sim scenario and confirm a clean scorecard
before opening the PR, so the engine change is proven against the real gate loop, not just unit tests.

### The CORE SURFACE makes that requirement automatic (HERD-577)

Where `CORE_SURFACE_GLOB` is armed, the paragraph above stops being advice and becomes a **gate**:
a PR whose diff matches the glob cannot merge without a green sandbox-sim scorecard, and core PRs
merge **one at a time** (the second holds with a calm `core diff · serialized behind PR #N` row until
the first lands). Ask the glob rather than guessing:

```
bash {{SCRIPTS_DIR}}/core-surface.sh paths     <worktree>   # the core paths this diff touches (rc 1 = not core)
bash {{SCRIPTS_DIR}}/core-surface.sh scenarios <worktree>   # the sim scenario(s) that diff must prove
```

**Spawn rule — do this at task-spec time, not at gate time.** Before you spawn, run `core-surface.sh
scenarios` against the item's intended file surface (or read the glob in `.herd/config`). If it comes
back non-empty, write BOTH of these into the task spec verbatim:

- *"Your diff touches the CORE SURFACE. Run `bash {{SCRIPTS_DIR}}/sim/<scenario>.sh --artifacts
  /tmp/<slug>-sim` yourself and read back `result: pass` from its `scorecard.json` BEFORE `gh pr
  create`; paste the scenario name + result into the PR body. The merge gate re-runs it — a red
  scorecard bounces you on the `coresim` rail exactly like a red healthcheck."*
- *"Core PRs SERIALIZE. If another core PR is open, yours will hold — blessed but not merged — until
  that one lands, then re-gate against the new base. That hold is expected, not a stall."*

Two coordinator consequences worth planning around: **do not spawn two core-surface items in
parallel** expecting both to land in one window (they will queue — sequence them, or accept the
latency), and when a core PR sits blessed-and-unmerged, check the console row before diagnosing a
wedged watcher — `core diff · serialized behind PR #N` means the engine is working as designed.

## Pipeline throughput & scaling

GROUNDED 2026-08-05 (HERD-530): an 8-builder fleet starved both gates because none of this lived
in the rendered skill — every seat had to re-derive it live from the engine. Read this before
spawning a large fleet, not after the gates back up.

**Lifecycle + diagnosis — `herd why` FIRST.** Every PR moves INTAKE → HEALTH → REVIEW → BLESSED →
merge; a `herd/gates Expected — Waiting` status is not itself a stall. Run `herd why <pr#>` (see
*Diagnose a gate* above) before touching anything. Two queued-not-stalled signatures to recognize
on sight:

- **`health-check · queued (N ahead)`** — slot starvation, not a hang: in-flight suites already
  fill the `HEALTH_CONCURRENCY` cap. A candidate with `health_pending` but no `healthcheck_started`
  in its journal hasn't started running yet — it's waiting its turn, not stuck.
- **A 0-byte suite log with a live, CPU-burning bats descendant** — RUNNING, not wedged. Confirm a
  live descendant before treating a silent log as dead (`HEALTH_EARLY_REAP_SECS` governs this).

**The three throughput knobs.**

| Knob | Where | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `REVIEW_CONCURRENCY` | `herd config set --local REVIEW_CONCURRENCY <n>` | max parallel pre-merge reviews (validated key) |
| `HEALTH_CONCURRENCY` | `herd config set --local HEALTH_CONCURRENCY <n>` | max parallel healthcheck suites (validated key) |
| `HEALTHCHECK_SUITE_WORKERS` | `export` in `.herd/config.local` | env-only, not a `config set` key — bounds bats' intra-suite `--jobs` fan-out |

Use `--local` in this SHARED checkout so the committed baseline stays clean for every operator;
`config set` auto-restarts the watcher for a watcher-affecting key, so a raised cap takes effect
immediately (see *Workflow settings* above).

**Scaling rule.** `REVIEW_CONCURRENCY`/`HEALTH_CONCURRENCY` default for a 2–3 builder herd — raise
both to match fleet size BEFORE spawning a large one, not after the gates back up.

**`HEALTH_SOURCE` — the knob that removes the health queue entirely (HERD-578).** The three knobs
above all divide a fixed local capacity; this one changes WHERE the verdict comes from.

| Value | What the watcher does |
|---|---|
| `local` (default) | dispatches the local healthcheck suite per `(pr, sha)`, serialized behind `HEALTH_CONCURRENCY` — that run is the verdict |
| `ci` | dispatches NOTHING locally and reads the PR's OWN GitHub Actions conclusion for its exact head sha as the verdict |

Under `ci` there is no `health-check · queued (N ahead)` row to diagnose, because no local slot is
consumed at all — PR gate latency collapses to CI's own wall-clock. The five mappings, all from one
shared implementation (`pysrc/herd/ci_verdict.py`, read by the watcher rail AND the main-health CI
leg AND `CI_FAST_BOUNCE`, so they can never disagree):

- **green** checks-run for the head sha → `CLEAN`, merges through the ordinary rails.
- **red naming a real test** → `CODEERROR`, bounced on the SAME `kind=health` rail (same once-guard,
  same `REFIX_MAX_ROUNDS` budget) `HEALTHCHECK_AUTOFIX` uses for a local red — one rail, not two —
  with the test CI named carried into the re-task prompt.
- **pending / cancelled-chain** → WAIT, never a red (the row is qualified with the cancelled count).
- **platform-infra red** (Actions outage: *Failed to resolve action download info*, *Service
  Unavailable*, a job never acquired by a runner) → `WAIT` + ONE bounded, journaled
  `gh run rerun --failed`, and NEVER a `CODEERROR`. **Do not re-task a builder for one of these** —
  `ci_infra_transient` in the journal means the platform failed, not the diff.
- **CI absent / gh unreachable** → falls back to the local suite and journals `ci_health_fallback`.
  A fleet still burning health slots under `HEALTH_SOURCE=ci` is this line: check it before assuming
  the lever is inert.

Set it with `herd config set --local HEALTH_SOURCE ci`, and only in a repo whose CI genuinely runs
the same suite on every PR head sha (an authenticated `gh` with Actions read + rerun access is
required). The verdict is a per-tick RECONCILED READ — polled fresh each tick, never a dispatched
collector that can die holding it — so a watcher restart mid-gate costs one re-read, nothing more.

**The spawn-gate formula.** A lane HOLDS a new spawn when in-flight builders already exceed
`REVIEW_CONCURRENCY + SPAWN_AHEAD` and the review pipeline is saturated. `spawn.sh` queues a held
spawn durably — nothing is lost — and the watcher drains the queue as slots free. `HERD_FORCE_SPAWN=1`
(or a leading `--force`) bypasses the hold for a genuinely urgent item and journals the bypass.

**Queue the whole plan, not just the next item** (`INTENT_QUEUE=on`, HERD-630). The spawn queue has
always externalized spawn EXECUTION; what still waited on a live coordinator turn was spawn
SELECTION. With the lever armed you enqueue the ranked plan ONCE and the watcher drains it as slots
free, whether or not this seat is between turns:

    HERD_ITEM_REF=<id> HERD_SPAWN_PRIO=10 bash {{SCRIPTS_DIR}}/spawn.sh <slug> feature "<task>"

- **`HERD_SPAWN_PRIO=<n>` is a BAND, lowest first**, FIFO by enqueue order within a band. Unmarked
  intents share a default band, so a queue where you published no priorities is exactly today's FIFO
  — arming the lever alone re-orders nothing. Leave gaps (10/20/30) so you can slot work between
  bands later without restamping the queue.
- **The queue consumes an order; it never computes one.** Ranking the backlog is yours. Re-ordering
  mid-drain is a re-read of the whole program — a coordinator act, not something to delegate.
- **Stale intents refuse themselves.** Before launching, the drain re-reads the item's tracker state:
  an item that shipped since you queued it is skipped (`spawn_skipped reason=re-ground`) and the
  drain moves to the next candidate — no worktree, branch or agent. An intent that sat undrained past
  `INTENT_TTL` (default 1 day) does not launch at all: it ESCALATES to a loud `spawn intents`
  needs-you row, because a builder run against a day-old plan costs a full run plus a bounced PR.
- **Clear escalations, don't ignore them.** `herd intents` lists them; re-queue what still matters,
  then `herd intents ack <n>` (or `ack all`). A row you never touch retires itself after the normal
  2 h retention, journaling `spawn_intent_retired` with its ref.
- **Countermand before it claims:** `bash {{SCRIPTS_DIR}}/spawn-step.sh cancel <intent-id>` retires a
  still-pending intent atomically (exit 3 means the drain already claimed it — countermand the
  BUILDER instead: close the PR, retire the worktree, release the claim).
- **NOT multi-seat safe yet.** Each watcher sizes its spawn budget from its own worktree roster, so
  two seats draining one pool can each admit up to their own cap. Arm it on one seat until Phase 4
  leases the slot through the machine-wide capacity tenant.

**Standing duty.** When engine mechanics or workflow behavior change, updating the affected skill
template is part of FINISHING that change — a stale section here is a rail a seat will follow into
a wrong action.

## Troubleshooting: setup problems or a wrong-rendering pane → `herd doctor` FIRST

When something in the control room looks wrong at the environment level — a command misbehaves on a
fresh or Windows machine, or a pane renders wrong (e.g. the backlog pane shows raw markdown / literal
`**` and backticks instead of the pretty view) — **run `herd doctor` before anything else.** It is a
one-pass dependency check that reports every problem at once with per-platform install hints:

    herd doctor

Read its output by tier:

- **Hard deps block.** A missing/broken REQUIRED dep (git, gh + auth, claude, python3, herdr) is a
  ✗ — `herd init` and the lanes cannot proceed until you install/fix it. Follow the printed `fix:` hint.
- **Soft deps each degrade ONE named feature** (a ⚠, never a block) — install the tool to restore it:
  - `glow` missing → the backlog pane renders **raw markdown** instead of the pretty view.
  - `shellcheck` missing → the healthcheck **skips shell lint** (`bash -n` still runs).
  - `bats` missing → the healthcheck runs the `*.sh` **test suite directly** instead of via bats.

A degraded pane is almost always a missing soft dep, not a code bug — `herd doctor` names which one and
how to install it. (Opt-in: set `DOCTOR_STARTUP_HINT=on` to have startup surface missing soft deps for
you on every `herd reload` / coordinator launch.)

## Routing: APP bug vs HERD-ENGINE bug (read every time)

Every reported problem routes to ONE of two places:

- **APP bug** — the symptom is in THIS project's own code (a wrong number, a broken tab, a
  connector failing). → spin a **worktree in this project** (the normal feature/quick lane above).
- **HERD-ENGINE bug** — the symptom is in the workflow itself (a lane mislabels state, the watcher
  races, the resolver guesses, a script leaks another project's assumptions). → **escalate OUT to
  the engine repo (`{{HERD_REPO}}`)**; do NOT patch it in this clone (the fix would be lost on the next
  `herd upgrade` and never reach other consumers). File it:
      herd report "<symptom + which lane>"
  (opens a `gh issue` on `{{HERD_REPO}}`, stamped with this project + the lane + version pin).

## Update the engine

When a fix has landed upstream in the engine, or the user asks "how do I get the latest engine
fix", run:

    herd update

It pulls HERDKIT_HOME (fast-forward only), shows the incoming delta, re-renders the coordinator
skill preserving `.herd/config` answers, and reloads the workspace — all in one step. It refuses
if the engine checkout is dirty or builders are mid-flight; pass `--force` to proceed anyway.

**After an engine update**, an update can add new config keys the engine now understands but this
project has not set — it silently rides their built-in defaults. `herd update`/`herd upgrade` print
the summary automatically, or run it anytime:

    herd config sync   # READ-ONLY: lists each engine key missing from .herd/config, its default + when to set it

For **each missing key the operator cares about**, interview them with **AskUserQuestion** (show the
default and its when-to-surface guidance), then apply the chosen value with `herd config set <KEY>
<VALUE>`. **Never auto-write** — `herd config sync` only reports; the operator decides, and only the
validated `herd config set` ever changes `.herd/config`. A key they don't care about stays on its
default, untouched.

