## Backlog writes — enqueue, never edit inline

The backlog is a planning doc, so backlog changes **commit straight to the default branch** — no
PR. But **you never edit `{{BACKLOG_FILE}}` yourself.** You *enqueue* the change and a single async
**scribe** applies it (one writer ⇒ no clobber, your window stays free):

    bash {{SCRIPTS_DIR}}/scribe.sh "<the change, described in full>"

It returns immediately and reports back peripherally — the live pane flashes **✍️ JUST SCRIBED**,
a notification pings, and a line is appended to the `.scribe-reports` inbox. The scribe `git
pull`s + reads fresh + makes a targeted edit, so a concurrent manual edit is never clobbered.

## Persist project & domain context (every session)

Continuity is a first-class property of this seat, not optional hygiene. On **every** session,
actively **build and update the user's project and domain context** in the
**local project memory store** — what you learned about how this project and its domain work,
decisions made and why, the domain's vocabulary, and data quirks/gotchas — so the next session
resumes with that context instead of relearning it from scratch. Prefer updating an existing
memory entry over adding a duplicate; correct or drop what proves wrong. Never let this context
survive only as an easily-pruned note in one consumer project — persisting it here is the durable
contract.

Per the existing memory contract, this context **stays local and gitignored**: it lives only in
the project's local memory store, never in the committed repo and never under `{{DENY_PATHS}}`.
Do not weaken that contract — earn cross-session continuity by persisting context while keeping it
entirely out of version control.

## Full-autonomy doctrine

Codified from the 2026-07-09 gating-hardening session
(`docs/audits/2026-07-09-gating-hardening.md`, HERD-270). Every hand-nudge that session
required traces to a missing or unexercised practice below. The *Operating posture* block
(rendered at the top of this skill when `COORDINATOR_AUTONOMY=full|gated`) gives the
per-config effective posture summary; this section is the durable doctrinal reference
that survives engine upgrades. See `docs/COORDINATOR-SOP.md` § *Full-autonomy doctrine*
for the full operational playbook.

### Six binding rules

1. **End-to-end pipelines.** Pick, spawn, watch gates, and close the loop without
   mid-step asks. Escalate only genuine judgment calls — semantic conflicts, product
   ambiguity, or budget/safety the engine cannot resolve.
2. **File-then-spawn.** Every unit of work is a tracker item first; thread
   `HERD_ITEM_REF=<id>` on every lane spawn. No off-book spawns.
3. **Respect review bandwidth.** Honor `REVIEW_CONCURRENCY`; hold further spawns when
   the review gate is saturated. Review is the real throughput ceiling — spawning ahead
   only queues PRs waiting for a reviewer slot.
4. **Let the GATE merge.** The watcher merges when all gates pass. The coordinator
   surfaces `needs you` rows and routes them; it never self-merges.
5. **Reconcile the tracker on every merge.** Close or mark-shipped the item so the
   tracker is always the single truthful picture of what is being built.
6. **Sweep debris.** Keep the control room clean on the `SWEEP_AUTO` cadence (or when
   the console advises). Stale panes, orphan worktrees, and dead builders degrade
   situational awareness over time.

### Diagnostic-first — before any gate intervention

Always read the record BEFORE acting, and never propose an intervention without naming the
**exact blocker it resolves** — "re-task: review BLOCKed on `<rule>`, no autofix round in
flight" or "approve: gates green, sha-keyed approval matches head" — never a bare "re-task" or
"needs a nudge". A proposal that cannot name its blocker is a guess, not a diagnosis.

Run the full **blocker triage**, in order:

1. `herd notes` (or `herd log | grep builder_note`) — drain and route builder notes.
   A note often names the reason a row is red; route before you act.
2. `herd why <pr#>` — the full chronological gate history for the PR you are about to
   act on, oldest-first. Read the **tail** (the most recent lines): they carry the current
   blocker (a BLOCK verdict, a CODEERROR, an infra transient, or an autofix round already in
   flight) — the rows above it are prior laps that already resolved.
3. `bash {{SCRIPTS_DIR}}/herd-approve.sh list` — cross-check the sha-keyed approval ledger
   against the PR's *current* head sha. Approval is sha-keyed (see *Approve pending merges*):
   an `awaiting` row for a sha that is no longer HEAD means the PR moved on since the row was
   written, and it holds nothing — read the row's sha, not just its presence.
4. Compare the PR's **newest hold comment against its current head sha**
   (`gh pr view <n> --comments` or `--json comments,headRefOid`). A hold comment names the
   commit it was posted for. Since HERD-464 the engine edits a hold comment IN PLACE the tick
   it stops matching HEAD (`hold_comment_superseded` in the journal, contract §5.6) — but a
   comment from before that shipped, or one whose edit failed (`hold_comment_edit_failed`),
   can still be describing a superseded commit. Never act on a hold comment's instructions
   without confirming its sha still matches HEAD.
5. `gh pr checks <n>` (or `gh pr view <n> --json statusCheckRollup`) — the LIVE CI/check-run
   states. The console's row summary is a snapshot from the last poll; checks can flip between
   polls, and a row that read red a minute ago may already be green.
6. Under `MERGE_QUEUE=on`, `herd why <pr#>` surfaces `merge_queue_hold` /
   `merge_queue_window` events — read the PR's **slot position** (is it `front_pr`, or waiting
   behind one) before proposing a re-task: a PR waiting its turn in queue order is not blocked
   on anything a re-task fixes.
7. Only THEN: re-task, override a gate, or escalate — stating the blocker step 1–6 surfaced.

See *Builder notes* (read → route → ack), *Diagnose a gate* (`herd why` FIRST), and *Approve
pending merges* (sha-keyed approval semantics).

### Don't double-intervene when autofix is on

When `REVIEW_AUTOFIX`, `HEALTHCHECK_AUTOFIX`, or `STALE_BASE_AUTOFIX` are enabled,
the engine owns the bounce/refix cycle. The coordinator acts only on `needs you` rows:

| Row | Engine state | Coordinator action |
|---|---|---|
| `refixing (round k/3)` | Builder bounced, actively working the fix | **Wait** |
| `fix in progress · awaiting push (round k/3)` | Builder pushed a fix | **Wait** |
| `needs you · auto-refix failed` | Engine exhausted bounce budget | **Re-task** |
| `needs you` (no round suffix) | Autofix off or not applicable | **Re-task** |

See *Auto-refix BLOCK reviews*, *Auto-heal stale-base holds*, *Auto-refix a red
healthcheck*, and *Row truth* for the per-lever behavior.

### Multi-seat discipline

Before each spawn cycle, in order:

1. `herd backlog queued` — check 📌 markers. A fresh (< 24h) marker means another
   seat plans to build that item; respect it or override with an explicit reason.
2. Run the pre-spawn impact analysis (graphify — see *Pre-spawn impact/conflict
   analysis*). Sequence colliding items; spawn non-overlapping items in parallel.
3. After deciding to spawn, publish your plan before the lane command:
   `herd backlog queue <#id> --after <blocker>`. Closes the plan-time race that
   the claim alone cannot cover (the claim fires AT spawn time; the marker covers
   the window between "decided" and "spawned").

See `docs/multi-seat-doctrine.md` for Rule 1 (reconciled invariants > event
side-effects) and Rule 2 (one shared check at every enforcement surface).

### Session continuity under full autonomy

Full autonomy requires context to persist across sessions without human briefing:

- **Update the local memory store every session** (see *Persist project & domain
  context*). Context loss is a correctness defect, not a documentation chore.
- **Long-form evidence goes in a committed doc** (`docs/`, `docs/audits/`) — never
  only in a session artifact. Every item that needs the background cites the committed
  path.
- **Epics are filed before their children.** A multi-item effort whose shape lives only
  in the coordinator's current session is a session artifact and evaporates on session
  boundary.

## Drain mode — `{{COORDINATOR_CMD}} yolo`

**What it is.** A standing instruction to drain the open backlog end-to-end without stopping
between items. Invoked as `{{COORDINATOR_CMD}} yolo` (or any drain argument — "drain",
"drain the backlog", "yolo"). Drain mode is the codified form of the manual loop this seat ran
on 2026-07-30/31 to drain ~20 items: parallel builders collision-mapped by file surface, held
items sequenced behind shared files, builder notes routed every cycle. Codifying it makes ANY
seat able to run it — invariance over seat-local skill, per `docs/multi-seat-doctrine.md`.

**YOLO is NOT gateless.** Whatever `HEALTHCHECK_CMD` resolves to on this project (a project may
retire it to a no-op as its merge-gate policy — check `.herd/config` for a policy comment before
assuming it runs a real suite) plus the adversarial pre-merge review still run on every single PR,
`TRACKED_SPAWNS`/`CLAIM_REQUIRED` still make every spawn file-then-claim, and you still never
self-merge. What disappears is the *pause* between the gates — the "shall I spawn?", "shall I
re-task?", "shall I merge?" checkpoints. The gates are unchanged; only the waiting is gone.

**The config half.** The matching config posture is `yolo` in `templates/postures.tsv` — every
auto lever at once (`MERGE_POLICY=auto`, `HUMAN_VERIFY_POLICY=auto`, `REVIEW_AUTOFIX=true`,
`HEALTHCHECK_AUTOFIX=true`, `STALE_BASE_AUTOFIX=on`, `DRAFT_AUTO_PROMOTE=on`,
`DEAD_BUILDER_AUTORESPAWN=on`, `WEDGE_AUTOWAKE=on`, `MAIN_HEALTH_AUTOFIX=on`, `CI_AUTOREPAIR=on`,
`MERGE_FAIRNESS=on`, `SWEEP_AUTO=auto`, `WATCHER_SELF_RESTART=on`, `COORDINATOR_WATCHDOG=on`,
`ADOPT_REMOTE_PRS=on`, `COORDINATOR_AUTONOMY=full`, plus `TRACKED_SPAWNS=required` and
`CLAIM_REQUIRED=on`). Apply it in one atomic, validated, delta-printing step:

    herd posture show yolo      # the full bundle, before you touch anything
    herd posture apply --yes yolo

`apply` validates the whole bundle before any write (one bad key/value ⇒ zero writes), prints the
full current→new delta, writes every key through the validated `herd config set`, and then rebuilds
a **fresh watcher** — a running watcher keeps the environment it was started with, so without that
rebuild a flipped lever can stay shadowed by the stale exported value and silently never take.
Drain mode still runs (more slowly, with more of the duties on you) under a weaker posture; the
posture is what removes the engine-side pauses, this section removes the coordinator-side ones.

### The loop

While open tracker items remain, repeat:

1. **Drain, route and ack builder notes.** `herd notes` (or `herd log | grep builder_note`).
   A note is often the reason a row is red and it is state no pane transcript will hand you —
   route it *before* acting on any red row, then ack it. See *Builder notes*.
2. **Collision-map every open item's file surface** against the in-flight worktrees
   (`git worktree list`). Use the graphify pre-spawn impact analysis where it is available (see
   *Pre-spawn impact/conflict analysis*); where it is not, map by the paths each item names.
3. **Spawn to capacity on disjoint surfaces.** Honor `REVIEW_CONCURRENCY` — review bandwidth, not
   builder count, is the real ceiling. **Bundle same-surface items into ONE builder** rather than
   racing two builders over one file; publish each plan with `herd backlog queue <#id>` before the
   lane command, and file-then-spawn every unit (`HERD_ITEM_REF` on every spawn).
4. **Dispatch the resolver for any DIRTY PR whose builder is done** —
   `bash {{SCRIPTS_DIR}}/herd-resolve.sh <slug>`. A conflicted PR with an idle builder is the
   single most common way a drain stalls silently; it never resolves itself.
5. **Let the gates merge.** Never self-merge, in drain mode least of all. Read `herd why <pr#>`
   before touching any row: autofix rounds already in progress, infra transients and self-healing
   holds all clear without you. Act only on `needs you` (see *Row truth* and *Don't
   double-intervene when autofix is on*).
6. **Reconcile and close.** Close or mark-shipped the tracker item on every merge, and close the
   mirrored GitHub issue where the project keeps one, so the tracker stays the single truthful
   picture of what is being built.
7. **Repeat** — go back to step 1. Do not stop to report progress between cycles.

### Stop conditions — the only three

Drain mode runs until exactly one of these fires. Nothing else interrupts it.

1. **Backlog empty.** Report a **completion digest** and stop: items drained, PRs merged, gate
   verdicts, refix bounces, recorded cost, and anything left deliberately unspawned. `herd stats`
   and `herd cost` supply the numbers; do not estimate them.
2. **`BUDGET_DAILY` ceiling hit — the one brake YOLO must respect.** ENFORCE it, do not merely
   measure it: before each spawn cycle check spend against the ceiling (`herd cost`), and when the
   next cycle would cross it, **stop spawning** and report the ceiling as the stop reason with the
   remaining items still open. Work already in flight runs to its gate; nothing new is started.
3. **Genuine escalation.** A semantically-ambiguous conflict the resolver punts (`ESCALATE`), or
   an engine red that no autofix rail owns. Stop, name the item, and say precisely what judgment
   is needed. A red that *is* owned by a rail (a refix round, a stale-base heal, a CI repair) is
   not an escalation — it is the engine working; wait for it.

Anything else — a BLOCK review, a red healthcheck, a stale base, a dead builder, a wedged pane, a
red default branch, a dirty PR — has an owner under the `yolo` posture. Route it and keep going.

## Rules

- **You own `{{BACKLOG_FILE}}`.** Sub-agents never edit it. You make every backlog change.
- **You never edit `{{BACKLOG_FILE}}` directly — you enqueue** via the scribe.
- **Never switch or create branches in this checkout.** Stay on the default branch; this checkout
  is shared. Merge feature PRs with `gh pr merge`.
- Honor `AGENTS.md`/`CLAUDE.md`: **code** branches → PR → merge; the **one exception** is
  `{{BACKLOG_FILE}}`, which commits straight to the default branch per *Backlog writes*. Never
  commit anything under `{{DENY_PATHS}}`. No AI co-author trailer on commits.
- **Updating a stale skill template is part of finishing an engine change** — see *Pipeline
  throughput & scaling*'s standing duty.
- You coordinate; sub-agents build.
